WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:00.900 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Our next guest is

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.068 align:start size:94% position:6%
an Emmy and Academy
Award-winning writer,

00:00:02.068 --> 00:00:04.604 align:start size:94% position:6%
producer, and director
behind movies like "Vice,"

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:05.739 align:start size:94% position:6%
"The Big Short,"
and "Anchorman."

00:00:05.739 --> 00:00:09.509
His podcast, "Death at the Wing:
How Drugs, Tragedy,

00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:12.312 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the Reagan Revolution
Defined a Decade of Basketball

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:14.914 align:start size:94% position:6%
is available
on Apple Podcasts, Spotify

00:00:14.914 --> 00:00:16.316 align:start size:94% position:6%
or wherever
you get your podcasts.

00:00:16.316 --> 00:00:18.284 align:start size:94% position:6%
Please welcome back
to the show Adam McKay.

00:00:18.284 --> 00:00:22.956 align:start size:94% position:6%
How are you, Adam?
-Mr. Meyers, how are you, sir?

00:00:22.956 --> 00:00:23.857 size:78% position:39% line:92%
-I'm doing very well.

00:00:23.857 --> 00:00:25.425 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's always nice
to speak to you.

00:00:25.425 --> 00:00:29.062 align:start size:94% position:6%
We had the same job,
different times.

00:00:29.062 --> 00:00:30.430 line:92%
We were head writers at "SNL."

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:33.666
But I wanted to ask you
about a different title you had,

00:00:33.666 --> 00:00:37.337 align:start size:94% position:6%
which you were the only person
ever to have that title.

00:00:37.337 --> 00:00:39.639 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think you know
what I'm talking about.

00:00:39.639 --> 00:00:42.175 align:start size:94% position:6%
Give us the backstory.
-Yeah.

00:00:42.175 --> 00:00:46.112 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, I was head writer at "SNL"
for a couple years.

00:00:46.112 --> 00:00:49.382 size:78% position:39%
Had a wonderful time,
very challenging job.

00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:52.786 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I kind of thought,
"Maybe it's time to move on,

00:00:52.786 --> 00:00:56.322 align:start size:94% position:6%
and, you know,
in a very friendly way,

00:00:56.322 --> 00:00:58.691 line:92%
and my manager at the time

00:00:58.691 --> 00:01:00.527 align:start size:94% position:6%
a guy by the name
of Jimmy Miller said, you know

00:01:00.527 --> 00:01:04.931 align:start size:94% position:6%
"If you're gonna quit,
make an unreasonable demand.

00:01:04.931 --> 00:01:09.436 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I said, "Well, I reall
want to direct films,

00:01:09.436 --> 00:01:13.173
so I would love to have a budget
to do short films.

00:01:13.173 --> 00:01:18.278
And I really don't like going to
all these production meetings,

00:01:18.278 --> 00:01:19.512 align:start size:94% position:6%
so I don't want to go
to the meetings,

00:01:19.512 --> 00:01:23.917
and I want a pay raise, and
want to name my own credit

00:01:23.917 --> 00:01:26.886 align:start size:94% position:6%
And even Jimmy was, like,
"That's a lot."

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:30.156 align:start size:94% position:6%
And Lorne was like,
"Okay. You got it."

00:01:30.156 --> 00:01:34.594 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so my credit
for my last two years

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:37.163 line:92%
was "Coordinator of Falconry."

00:01:37.163 --> 00:01:37.764 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:41.534 align:start size:94% position:6%
And if you look at it,
they ran it towards

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:43.603 align:start size:94% position:6%
the end of the credits.
-Sure.

00:01:43.603 --> 00:01:47.140 size:81% position:41%
-But a friend of mine
got a frame grab of it

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:50.176 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I have framed,
but as far as I know --

00:01:50.176 --> 00:01:52.112 size:84% position:42%
and we'd have to dig
through the archives --

00:01:52.112 --> 00:01:55.782 size:84% position:42%
I believe I'm the only
Coordinator of Falconry

00:01:55.782 --> 00:01:56.783 size:81% position:41% line:92%
the show has ever had.

00:01:56.783 --> 00:01:59.686 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, any time there were
loose falcons

00:01:59.686 --> 00:02:01.654 align:start size:94% position:6%
that were gonna
fly in the studio...

00:02:01.654 --> 00:02:04.524 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-...bring back prey to someone

00:02:04.524 --> 00:02:07.494 align:start size:94% position:6%
with that leather glove on
they called me.

00:02:07.494 --> 00:02:11.397 align:start size:94% position:6%
Now, unfortunately, that never
happened in the two years.

00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:13.066 align:start size:94% position:6%
I never got that call.
-Right.

00:02:13.066 --> 00:02:17.604
-But I was ready and trained
and I was ready to support "SNL"

00:02:17.604 --> 00:02:18.771 size:75% position:38% line:92%
in any way possible.

00:02:18.771 --> 00:02:20.507 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So, this podcast
about basketball,

00:02:20.507 --> 00:02:24.077 align:start size:94% position:6%
specifically basketball
in the '80s and how it sort of

00:02:24.077 --> 00:02:28.414
intertwined the politics of that
era with the excess of that era,

00:02:28.414 --> 00:02:32.318
and how, for a lot of player
in the league at the time,

00:02:32.318 --> 00:02:35.188 line:92%
it had very tragic outcomes.

00:02:35.188 --> 00:02:38.391 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah. It's a story
that I've been tracking.

00:02:38.391 --> 00:02:41.628 align:start size:94% position:6%
I've been an NBA fan
or decades,

00:02:41.628 --> 00:02:43.563 align:start size:94% position:6%
and through talking
to my friends,

00:02:43.563 --> 00:02:45.365 size:75% position:38%
we were always doing
the "what if" game.

00:02:45.365 --> 00:02:48.835 align:start size:94% position:6%
We're like,
"What if Len Bias had played

00:02:48.835 --> 00:02:52.205 align:start size:94% position:6%
What if
Drazen Petrovic had played?"

00:02:52.205 --> 00:02:54.440 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it was all these
great talents

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.109 line:92%
that all died very young.

00:02:56.109 --> 00:02:59.779 align:start size:94% position:6%
Benji Wilson,
Terry Furlow, Hank Gathers -

00:02:59.779 --> 00:03:01.548 line:92%
it's an incredibly long list

00:03:01.548 --> 00:03:04.284 align:start size:94% position:6%
And at a certain point, like
seven, eight years ago,

00:03:04.284 --> 00:03:05.351 line:92%
it was like, "Wait a minute.

00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:07.754 align:start size:94% position:6%
Why did all these guys
die in this period?

00:03:07.754 --> 00:03:10.924 align:start size:94% position:6%
I can't find this
in any other sport."

00:03:10.924 --> 00:03:14.260 align:start size:94% position:6%
So the fun of, you know,
a podcast is,

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:18.198 align:start size:94% position:6%
you can kind of dive into it
with the question intact.

00:03:18.198 --> 00:03:20.200 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, if you sold this
as a TV series,

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.435 align:start size:94% position:6%
you'd have to already answer
the question.

00:03:22.435 --> 00:03:26.639
But I was lucky enough, I go
these incredible producers

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:29.542
and we just dove in,
and what ended up happening was,

00:03:29.542 --> 00:03:32.045 align:start size:94% position:6%
it became this portrait
of the '80s,

00:03:32.045 --> 00:03:36.849
of the explosion of the NBA,
media, the Reagan Revolution

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:40.553 align:start size:94% position:6%
and all these kind of amazin
stories of these players.

00:03:40.553 --> 00:03:45.058
-One of the things that I thin
people who are hyper-aware o

00:03:45.058 --> 00:03:47.026 align:start size:94% position:6%
what professional athletes
get paid today

00:03:47.026 --> 00:03:51.264
might be surprised to know
is how many athletes of that era

00:03:51.264 --> 00:03:54.767 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the '70s and '80s
had second jobs.

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:57.770 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah. It's weird.
Like, you think of basketbal

00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:02.542 align:start size:94% position:6%
as this giant sport,
but in the '60s and '70s,

00:04:02.542 --> 00:04:04.444 line:92%
it was like the seventh sport.

00:04:04.444 --> 00:04:09.449
It was like, football, baseball,
like, even to some degree,

00:04:09.449 --> 00:04:13.753 align:start size:94% position:6%
bowling, boxing, hockey,
college basketball.

00:04:13.753 --> 00:04:17.423
So, Jerry West -- we interviewed
the great Jerry West,

00:04:17.423 --> 00:04:21.127 align:start size:94% position:6%
and he told me that he mad
$16,000 a year

00:04:21.127 --> 00:04:25.098
after he was drafted, and he was
like the number-two draft pick

00:04:25.098 --> 00:04:27.500 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I learned
from Jackie MacMullan,

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:30.270 align:start size:94% position:6%
the great sportswriter,
that Bob Cousy

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:33.806 size:88% position:44%
was a driving instructor
during the off season.

00:04:33.806 --> 00:04:35.742 align:start size:94% position:6%
And for people who don't know,
Cousy was, like,

00:04:35.742 --> 00:04:40.313 align:start size:94% position:6%
a transformative point guard
you know, Hall of Famer.

00:04:40.313 --> 00:04:44.217 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then, Tommy Heinsohn,
who was a forward

00:04:44.217 --> 00:04:45.952 line:92%
for the Celtics' great teams

00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:48.921 align:start size:94% position:6%
he was a waiter
during the off season,

00:04:48.921 --> 00:04:49.856 line:92%
but he would chain smoke.

00:04:49.856 --> 00:04:53.226 align:start size:94% position:6%
So he would come over to
the table with a Caesar salad,

00:04:53.226 --> 00:04:56.296 align:start size:94% position:6%
and he would just reek
of Pall Malls,

00:04:56.296 --> 00:04:59.999 align:start size:94% position:6%
and so they had to boot
Tommy Heinsohn.

00:04:59.999 --> 00:05:03.136 line:92%
And it kind of went on and on.

00:05:03.136 --> 00:05:06.539 line:92%
Bill Walton worked at the ASPCA.

00:05:06.539 --> 00:05:09.442 align:start size:94% position:6%
He was neutering animals,
and -- all right.

00:05:09.442 --> 00:05:12.845 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's clearly made up,
but the other ones are true.

00:05:12.845 --> 00:05:13.613 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:05:13.613 --> 00:05:18.484
-Hey, one thing, you know,
as we talk about, you know

00:05:18.484 --> 00:05:20.553 size:88% position:44% line:92%
legends of days gone by.

00:05:20.553 --> 00:05:23.623 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, I'm coming up in Chicago
as an improviser

00:05:23.623 --> 00:05:25.158 align:start size:94% position:6%
sort of in the era
right after you.

00:05:25.158 --> 00:05:30.029
And I would always hear tell -
it was of legendary status -

00:05:30.029 --> 00:05:33.700 line:92%
the shows that you and your --

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:35.702 size:84% position:42%
you know, your fellow
improvisers at the time

00:05:35.702 --> 00:05:39.939
would do, not in theaters, but
out on the streets of Chicago.

00:05:39.939 --> 00:05:41.641 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you have any memories
of the street shows

00:05:41.641 --> 00:05:44.210 align:start size:94% position:6%
you were doing as improvisers?
-Yeah.

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:46.212 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was
The Upright Citizens Brigade

00:05:46.212 --> 00:05:49.415
which was our mangy little group
in Chicago,

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:53.553 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it was Horatio Sanz,
it was Matt Besser,

00:05:53.553 --> 00:05:57.290 align:start size:94% position:6%
it was Ian Roberts.
Amy Poehler joined us.

00:05:57.290 --> 00:06:00.960 align:start size:94% position:6%
We had Rich Fulcher,
all these different people

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:02.695 align:start size:94% position:6%
and there was one show we di
that was called

00:06:02.695 --> 00:06:05.365 align:start size:94% position:6%
"The Conference
on the Future of Happiness."

00:06:05.365 --> 00:06:09.602 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, essentially, we were al
these self-help gurus

00:06:09.602 --> 00:06:12.505 align:start size:94% position:6%
who had promised that,
at the end of the show that,

00:06:12.505 --> 00:06:15.508 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, the fiction was,
every audience member

00:06:15.508 --> 00:06:18.745 align:start size:94% position:6%
had paid $5,000
to come to this conference

00:06:18.745 --> 00:06:22.582 align:start size:94% position:6%
and we had one sentence that
if you heard it,

00:06:22.582 --> 00:06:24.684 line:92%
you would get eternal happiness.

00:06:24.684 --> 00:06:29.822
And at one point, my character
stole the sentence, and run --

00:06:29.822 --> 00:06:33.860
ran out into the streets through
the real streets of Chicago,

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:37.630 align:start size:94% position:6%
and little did the audienc
know, my apartment was nearby,

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:40.199 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it was
a first-floor apartment.

00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:42.502 align:start size:94% position:6%
So they chased me
to the apartment.

00:06:42.502 --> 00:06:45.772 align:start size:94% position:6%
I busted into it like it wasn'
my apartment

00:06:45.772 --> 00:06:50.877 align:start size:94% position:6%
and proceeded to murder
Horatio Sanz, who was watching

00:06:50.877 --> 00:06:53.679 align:start size:94% position:6%
like, you know,
"Matlock" on his TV

00:06:53.679 --> 00:06:59.285 align:start size:94% position:6%
and still had the sentence
and then came out the other en

00:06:59.285 --> 00:07:03.456 align:start size:94% position:6%
of the walkway, and there wa
a car waiting for us

00:07:03.456 --> 00:07:06.692 align:start size:94% position:6%
with bikini models on it,
and it was playing

00:07:06.692 --> 00:07:10.963 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Everything is Beautiful"
blaring from the car.

00:07:10.963 --> 00:07:13.733 align:start size:94% position:6%
And all of us --
Besser, Ian, Horatio --

00:07:13.733 --> 00:07:15.935 size:88% position:44% line:92%
got on the car and said,

00:07:15.935 --> 00:07:18.504 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Do you want the sentence
of happiness?"

00:07:18.504 --> 00:07:21.073 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the whole crowd yelled
"Yes!"

00:07:21.073 --> 00:07:24.510
And we opened up a box,
and it was full of dough,

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:27.513 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then we whipped dough
at the audience,

00:07:27.513 --> 00:07:31.517 align:start size:94% position:6%
called them a bunch of suckers
And I'll never forget

00:07:31.517 --> 00:07:33.886 align:start size:94% position:6%
the main critic
from "The Chicago Reader,"

00:07:33.886 --> 00:07:35.488 align:start size:94% position:6%
which you remember, Seth,
that was, like,

00:07:35.488 --> 00:07:38.558 size:78% position:39%
one of the big places
to get reviewed in.

00:07:38.558 --> 00:07:40.593 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes.
-I think Jack Helbig

00:07:40.593 --> 00:07:42.462 align:start size:94% position:6%
was his name.
-He was the guy.

00:07:42.462 --> 00:07:46.065 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And I hit him dead center
right on his chest

00:07:46.065 --> 00:07:48.034 line:92%
with a big hunk of dough.

00:07:48.034 --> 00:07:50.570 size:84% position:42%
And then we drove away
with the bikini models,

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:54.040 align:start size:94% position:6%
leaving the audience, like
on some strange street

00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:59.378 align:start size:94% position:6%
and just ending the show.
-And how was Helbig's review

00:07:59.378 --> 00:08:02.415 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It was mixed.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:02.415 --> 00:08:03.349 align:start size:94% position:6%
-That's fair.
That's fair.

00:08:03.349 --> 00:08:06.352 align:start size:94% position:6%
Hey, thank you. It's alway
great to see you, Adam.

00:08:06.352 --> 00:08:08.554 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Death at the Wing"
is available on Apple Podcasts

00:08:08.554 --> 00:08:11.390 size:81% position:41%
Spotify, or wherever
you get your podcasts.

